What is Expanded Clay?
Expanded Clay is a lightweight aggregate produced by heating natural clay at high temperatures (typically between 1,100°C and 1,200°C) in a rotary kiln. This process causes the clay to expand, forming porous pellets with a hard outer shell and a honeycombed internal structure. The material is widely used in construction, horticulture, and water treatment due to its excellent insulation properties, durability, sustainability, lightweight characteristics, thermal insulation, and fire resistance.

